\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Nested conditional expectations compose}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{Let #{A} be a unital associative algebra and #{\omega} a linear
functional on #{A}. Let #{B\subset A} be a unital subalgebra. A
\newvocab{conditional expectation} of #{\omega} onto #{B} is a
\vocabk{normalized conditional functional}{fdip-00KV}
#{E:A\to B} satisfying the further identity #{\omega(E(a))=\omega(a)}.
When #{A} carries the involution of an
\vocabk{algebraic state}{fdip-0024}, #{\omega} is such a state, and
#{E(a^*)=E(a)^*}, the same identities give
#{\omega(c^*E(a^*a)c)=\omega((ac)^*(ac))} for #{c\in B}. No norm
on #{A} enters that identity.}

\p{Keep the Wick data of the [ring-level moment theorem](fdip-008N):
#{2} is a unit, #{Q} is symmetric and invertible, and #{E_T} denotes
the substitution map of that note onto a retained set #{T}, defined
whenever the eliminated block is invertible. For retained sets
#{R\subset R'}, write #{U'} for the complement of #{R'} and #{V}
for the coordinates in #{R'} but not in #{R}. Let #{S'} be the Schur complement of #{Q}
after eliminating #{U'}, and assume that #{Q_{U'U'}} and the block
#{(S')_{VV}} are invertible.}

\p{Then #{E_R} is a conditional expectation of #{\omega_Q} onto
#{A[x_R]}. The two maps compose,
##{E_R\circ E_{R'}=E_R.}
On the four-cycle of the moment note, the conditional expectation of
the displayed quartic lies in #{\mathbb Q[x_0,x_1]}, and the common
value of the two Wick functionals is #{32/75}. The residual
evaluations are normalized conditional functionals of weight #{1},
and the one-step evaluation equals their composite. This is the raw
identity of \ref{fdip-00KX} with #{w=z=t=1}.}

\proof{
\p{The constant #{1} does not involve the residual indeterminates, and
the Wick value of #{1} is #{1}, so #{E_R(1)=1}. A polynomial
#{g\in A[x_R]} is a coefficient for the residual Wick functional.
That functional is linear over #{A[x_R]}, hence
#{E_R(gf)=gE_R(f)}. The polynomial ring is commutative, so the two
sides of the bimodule identity agree. The moment theorem gives
#{\omega_Q(h)=\omega_Q(E_R(h))} for every polynomial #{h}. Taking
#{h=gf} yields
#{\omega_Q(gE_R(f))=\omega_Q(gf)}. The image lies in #{A[x_R]} by
that same theorem.}

\p{Apply the moment theorem to the partition #{(R',U')}. The residual
#{z'=x_{U'}+L'x_{R'}} is uncorrelated with #{x_{R'}}, and its
covariance is #{Q_{U'U'}^{-1}}. The same theorem, applied to the
invertible precision #{S'} and the partition #{(R,V)}, produces a
residual #{z_V} in the variables #{x_{R'}} whose covariance is
#{(S')_{VV}^{-1}} and which is uncorrelated with #{x_R}. Second
moments of polynomials in #{x_{R'}} agree under #{\omega_Q} and
under the Wick state of #{S'}, so these two covariances are the
moments of #{\omega_Q}. Since #{z_V} is linear in #{x_{R'}} and
#{z'} is uncorrelated with #{x_{R'}}, one has
#{\omega_Q(z_Vz')=0}.}

\p{The [moment theorem](fdip-008N) defines the Schur
complement after eliminating #{U'} by
#{S'=Q_{R'R'}-Q_{R'U'}Q_{U'U'}^{-1}Q_{U'R'}}. Its block
on #{V} is therefore
#{(S')_{VV}=Q_{VV}-Q_{VU'}Q_{U'U'}^{-1}Q_{U'V}}. The same
expression is the Schur complement of the invertible
block #{Q_{U'U'}} inside #{Q_{UU}}. That theorem says
that, once the eliminated block is invertible, the block
matrix is invertible if and only if that Schur complement
is. Both are invertible, so #{Q_{UU}} is invertible, and
the one-step map #{E_R} is defined. Substitute
##{x_V=B_Vx_R+z_V}
and then
##{x_{U'}=B'x_{R'}+z'.}
The second substitution expands to a retained linear form plus
#{B'_Vz_V+z'}. In residual coordinates the change of variables is
##{z_U=
\begin{pmatrix}I&0\\ B'_V&I\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}z_V\\ z'\end{pmatrix}.}
The triangular factor has determinant #{1} and an inverse over
#{A}. Both #{z_V} and #{z'} are uncorrelated with #{x_R}, so the
residual is uncorrelated with #{x_R}. Its retained coefficient is
therefore the regression of the moment theorem: the covariance
#{C_{RR}} is invertible, and
#{C(x_U,x_R)=BC_{RR}} determines that coefficient. The one-step
residual #{z_U} is this triangular image.}

\p{A pairing in the joint residual that joins #{z_V} to #{z'}
contributes #{0}. Every surviving pairing is a #{z_V} pairing times
a #{z'} pairing, so the joint Wick average is the iterated average.
The polynomial obtained after the two substitutions is the same
polynomial of #{z_U}, and Isserlis' rule depends only on the
pairwise covariances of those linear forms. Substitution of
#{x_V=B_Vx_R+z_V} is a ring homomorphism, and the residual Wick
functional is linear on coefficients, so the #{z'} average commutes
with that substitution. The average in #{z'} alone is
#{E_{R'}(f)}. Once that polynomial no longer involves #{z'}, the
one-step residual average uses only #{z_V}, with covariance
#{(S')_{VV}^{-1}}. On #{A[x_{R'}]} that average is #{E_R}. The
iterated average is therefore #{E_R(E_{R'}(f))}, and it equals
#{E_R(f)}.}
}

\p{The block factorization used above is the same matrix identity
recorded for the moment theorem. TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{Matrix.PosDef.gaussianCondCov} remains the real
positive-definite covariance, not this tower over a ring. No Mathlib
declaration was found for the Isserlis composition.}

\p{No norm on the algebra enters these identities, and they are not
a norm-one projection.}
